        <div class="gradient-box">
            <h2 id="compatibility-product-type">Chemical Compatibility by Product Type</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Gasket selection must always be tied to the formulation class rather than product category or market positioning. Even small changes in solvent blends can radically alter compatibility outcomes.</p>
        <h3>Solvent-Based Paints and Coatings</h3>
        <p>These formulations often contain aromatic hydrocarbons, ketones, or esters that aggressively attack many elastomers. Incorrect gasket selection leads to swelling and leakage.</p>
        <h3>Water-Based and Emulsion Systems</h3>
        <p>Water-based aerosols require elastomers that resist hydrolysis and maintain elasticity in polar environments. Hydrocarbon-compatible rubbers fail rapidly here.</p>
        <h3>Hydrocarbon Propellant Systems</h3>
        <p>Liquefied petroleum gases diffuse easily into many elastomers. Resistance to swelling and plasticizer extraction is essential. The underlying reason is polarity: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nitrile_rubber" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">nitrile rubber</a> owes its hydrocarbon resistance to the polar acrylonitrile content in the copolymer, and raising that content improves oil resistance while reducing low-temperature flexibility. Propellant choice therefore constrains gasket choice directly, as set out in our guide to <a href="/aerosol-propellants-explained-lpg-vs-dme-vs-hfc-vs-n2-vs-co2" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">aerosol propellants</a>.</p>
        <p>Typical compatibility trends:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>NBR</strong> — performs well with LPG but poorly with water</li>
            <li><strong>EPDM</strong> — performs well with water but poorly with LPG</li>
            <li><strong>FKM</strong> — performs well across most systems</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="gradient-box">
            <h2 id="testing">Valve Gasket Compatibility Testing</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Compatibility cannot be assumed from material datasheets alone. Testing under real formulation conditions is essential to avoid delayed field failures. Standardised classification and test methods for rubber materials are published by <a href="https://www.astm.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">ASTM International</a>, and equivalent international methods are maintained by <a href="https://www.iso.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">ISO</a>; working to a named method is what makes results comparable between two suppliers.</p>
        <p>Standard validation approaches include:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Immersion soak testing with dimensional measurement</li>
            <li>Compression set testing after chemical exposure</li>
            <li>Accelerated aging at elevated temperatures</li>
            <li>Pressure retention testing over time</li>
        </ul>
