        <div class="gradient-box">
            <h2 id="tropical-markets">Special Considerations for Indian and Tropical Markets</h2>
        </div>
        <p>High ambient temperatures accelerate chemical diffusion and elastomer aging. Gaskets that perform acceptably in temperate regions may fail prematurely in tropical climates.</p>
        <p>Environmental factors to consider include:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Elevated storage and transport temperatures</li>
            <li>Higher internal vapor pressure</li>
            <li>Longer distribution timelines</li>
            <li>Non-climate-controlled warehousing</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="gradient-box">
            <h2 id="prevention">Failure Prevention Through Correct Gasket Selection</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Preventing gasket-related failures requires treating gasket selection as a formulation compatibility decision, not a procurement exercise. Early testing and conservative material choices reduce downstream risk.</p>
        <p>Best-practice prevention steps include:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Matching elastomer chemistry to formulation polarity</li>
            <li>Testing with actual product, not surrogate solvents</li>
            <li>Accounting for climate and storage conditions</li>
            <li>Avoiding cost-driven downgrades without validation</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="gradient-box">
            <h2 id="references">External Technical References</h2>
        </div>
        <p>For further independent technical reading on elastomer compatibility and sealing systems:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><a href="https://www.parker.com/Literature/O-Ring%20Division%20Literature/ORD%205700.pdf">Parker O-Ring Handbook – Elastomer compatibility fundamentals</a></li>
            <li><a href="https://utexind.com/materials-resources/chemical-compatibility/">FKM & EPDM Chemical Compatibility Charts - Utex Industries</a></li>
            <li>ISO 815 and ASTM D395 — the compression set test methods referenced throughout this guide</li>
            <li>Where an aerosol is food-contact, the elastomer must also satisfy the rubber-article requirements administered by the <a href="https://www.fda.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: var(--primary); font-weight: 600;">US Food and Drug Administration</a></li>
        </ul>
        <div class="gradient-box">
            <h2 id="ecosystem">Understanding the Broader Aerosol Ecosystem</h2>
        </div>
        <p>Valve gaskets do not function in isolation. Their performance is directly linked to can materials, propellants, filling methods, corrosion behavior, and safety systems. A holistic understanding of the aerosol ecosystem is essential for designing reliable and compliant products.</p>
        <p>For more information about the Aerosol ecosystem, please read our comprehensive articles that delve into the intricacies of aerosol product development: Explore <a href="/how-aerosol-cans-work/">how aerosol cans work</a>, gain foundational knowledge with <a href="/aerosol-manufacturing-101-guide/">aerosol manufacturing 101</a>, understand corrosion challenges in <a href="/aerosol-can-corrosion-1/">part 1</a> and <a href="/aerosol-can-corrosion-2/">part 2</a>, master <a href="/services/product-formulation/">aerosol formulation</a> techniques, ensure safety through <a href="/aerosol-can-safety-explained/">aerosol can safety explained</a>, compare propellants in <a href="/aerosol-propellants-explained-lpg-vs-dme-vs-hfc-vs-n2-vs-co2/">aerosol propellants explained: LPG vs DME vs HFC vs N2 vs CO2</a>, and learn about operations in <a href="/how-contract-aerosol-filling-works/">how contract aerosol filling works</a>. Together, these resources provide a complete engineering view of aerosol product development and commercialization from concept to shelf.</p>

<!-- FAQ -->
<section class="section" id="faq" style="margin-top: 50px;">
  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
    <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">What does an aerosol valve gasket actually do?</summary>
    <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">The gasket is the elastomeric seal compressed between the valve cup and the can bead when the valve is crimped into place. It is the component that actually contains the internal pressure, because metal-to-metal contact alone cannot form a gas-tight joint. Every aerosol therefore depends on that ring of rubber staying resilient and chemically stable for the entire shelf life of the product.</div>
  </details>

  <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
    <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">Which gasket materials are commonly used in aerosol valves?</summary>
    <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">The common families are buna-N (nitrile), neoprene (polychloroprene), butyl rubber, and EPDM, with fluoroelastomers used for unusually aggressive formulations. Nitrile offers good resistance to hydrocarbon propellants and oils, while butyl and EPDM perform better with polar and water-based systems. No single elastomer suits every formulation, which is why material selection follows the product chemistry rather than a default.</div>
  </details>

  <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
    <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">How do I choose a gasket material for my formulation?</summary>
    <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Selection starts from the solvency of the concentrate and the propellant rather than from cost. A broadly useful rule is that chemically similar materials dissolve one another, so hydrocarbon-rich systems attack non-polar elastomers while polar and aqueous systems are better matched to butyl or EPDM. The candidate material is then confirmed by immersion testing in the actual finished formulation, never selected from a datasheet alone.</div>
  </details>

  <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
    <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">What is compression set and why does it matter?</summary>
    <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Compression set measures how much of its original thickness an elastomer fails to recover after being held compressed for a period at temperature. A gasket with high compression set gradually stops pushing back against the sealing faces, so the joint loses the contact force that keeps it gas-tight. It is one of the most useful predictors of whether a seal will still hold after months in a warm warehouse.</div>
  </details>

  <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
    <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">What causes an aerosol valve gasket to swell?</summary>
    <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">Swelling occurs when solvent or propellant molecules are absorbed into the polymer network and force the chains apart. Moderate swelling can temporarily improve sealing by increasing contact pressure, but excessive swelling distorts the gasket, extracts plasticiser, and permanently weakens it. Because the effect depends on the specific solvent and elastomer pairing, it must be assessed with the real formulation.</div>
  </details>

  <details style="border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-md); margin-bottom: 10px; background: #ffffff;">
    <summary style="font-weight: 600; padding: 14px 18px; cursor: pointer;">How is gasket compatibility tested before production?</summary>
    <div style="padding: 0 18px 16px; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.65;">The standard approach is immersion testing, where weighed gasket samples are held in the finished formulation at elevated temperature and then re-measured for change in mass, volume, and hardness. Standardised methods for classifying and testing rubber materials are published by ASTM International, which allows results to be compared consistently between suppliers. These bench results are then confirmed with filled cans held under accelerated storage conditions.</div>
  </details>
</section>

